  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x-9}$ and $g(x)=x^2$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $f(g(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{2}{x^2-9}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=\\frac{2}{g(x)-9}\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{2}{x^2-9}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $g(f(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{4}{(x-9)^2}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=(f(x))^2\\\\g(f(x))&=\\left(\\frac{2}{x-9}\\right)^2\\\\g(f(x))&=\\frac{4}{(x-9)^2}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find domains of $f(g(x))$ and $g(f(x))$. Are they the same?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. $f(g(x))$: $x\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-3,3\\}$. $g(f(x))$: $x\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{9\\}$.",
          "worked": "For $f(g(x))$, the denominator must be nonzero: $x^2-9\\ne0$, so $x\\ne-3,3$. Its domain is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-3,3\\}$.\n\nFor $g(f(x))$, the inner function requires $x\\ne9$; squaring adds no restriction. Its domain is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{9\\}$.\n\nThe domains are different.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      }
    ]
  },
